        "title": "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Area of a Quadrilateral"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's start by finding the area of $\u25b3ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\u25b3ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061{75}^{\u2218} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061{75}^{\u2218} \\\\  & =11.591\u2026 \\\\  & \u224812{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest integer**.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $\u25b3ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $\u25b3BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\u25b3BCD} & ={A}_{\u25b3ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =12-10 \\\\  & =2{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/b6/b69082ef86283bc842ce487b62901e8946ffff53ab8323a6fc846f68c5869aae.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $10{\\text{\u00a0cm}}^{2}.$   Then, the area of $\u25b3BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is{{field-1}}${\\text{cm}}^{2}.$",
